From: Worldshepherd OSS contribution workbench
Subject: [PATCH DRAFT] health: shorten recheck cadence while unhealthy
Upstream target: spiffe/spire#7236
Claims state: PATCH DRAFT / REQUIRES UPSTREAM TEST + MAINTAINER REVIEW

Problem
-------
SPIRE currently checks health once per second during initial startup, then once
per minute after every subsystem has first become ready and live. If a steady-
state check lands during a brief database or dependency blip, the cached state
can remain unhealthy for the full steady-state minute even after the dependency
has recovered.

This draft preserves the low-cost one-minute cadence while all cached health is
healthy. Once any steady-state check reports not-ready or not-live, subsequent
checks run at a shorter recovery cadence until all subsystems are healthy again.
This addresses stale-unhealthy recovery without making healthy clusters poll
continuously at high frequency.

The proposed 5-second recovery interval is intentionally reviewable/tunable; it
follows the range suggested in the issue discussion and is not claimed as a
final upstream default.

--- a/pkg/common/health/health.go
+++ b/pkg/common/health/health.go
@@
 const (
 	readyCheckInitialInterval = time.Second
+	readyCheckRecoveryInterval = 5 * time.Second
 	readyCheckInterval        = time.Minute
 )

--- a/pkg/common/health/cache.go
+++ b/pkg/common/health/cache.go
@@
 	go func() {
 		defer func() {
 			c.log.Debug("Finishing health checker")
 		}()
 
 		// Wait until initial ready + live state is achieved, then periodically check health at a longer interval
 		<-startSteadyStateHealthCheckCh
+		checkInterval := readyCheckInterval
 		for {
 			select {
-			case <-c.clk.After(readyCheckInterval):
+			case <-c.clk.After(checkInterval):
 			case <-ctx.Done():
 				return
 			}
 
 			checkFunc()
+
+			// Healthy steady state stays on the low-cost interval. Once any
+			// subsystem is unhealthy, recheck more quickly so a transient
+			// failure does not remain cached for a full minute after recovery.
+			checkInterval = readyCheckInterval
+			for _, status := range c.getStatuses() {
+				if !status.details.Ready || !status.details.Live {
+					checkInterval = readyCheckRecoveryInterval
+					break
+				}
+			}
 		}
 	}()
 }

